    return -ENOMEM;
}
```
```c
ahci_pdev = platform_device_alloc("ahci", -1);
if (!ahci_pdev)
    return -ENODEV;
```
```c
if (type == AHCI_IMX6Q) {
    imxpriv->gpr = syscon_regmap_lookup_by_compatible("fsl,imx6q-iomuxc-gpr");
    if (IS_ERR(imxpriv->gpr)) {
        dev_err(dev, "failed to find fsl,imx6q-iomux-gpr regmap\n");
        return PTR_ERR(imxpriv->gpr);
    }
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because when an error is encountered during the resource allocation or initialization process (e.g., memory allocation for `imxpriv`, allocation of `ahci_pdev`, or fetching the `gpr` register map), the function returns immediately without releasing any previously allocated resources. This leads to resource leaks, as the allocated memory or devices are not properly freed.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
ret = PTR_ERR(imxpriv->gpr);
goto err_out;
```
```c
err_out:
    platform_device_put(ahci_pdev);
    return ret;
```
Reason:
The fix involves changing the immediate return statements to set an error code (`ret`) and then jumping to the `err_out` label. This ensures that when an error occurs, the function will execute the cleanup code that releases any allocated resources (like `ahci_pdev`) before returning the error code, thus preventing resource leaks and ensuring stability in the driver.

